WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.488
 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:00:01.488 --> 00:00:01.988
Hey!

00:00:01.988 --> 00:00:02.303
Hey!

00:00:02.303 --> 00:00:02.803
Hey!

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.830
 Metallica's that family I
never had or always wanted

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.810
that I found in a band.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:17.145
ANNOUNCER: Artist of the
year goes to Metallica!

00:00:17.145 --> 00:00:20.430
We all know each other, and
we continue after 40 years

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.170
to learn more about each other.

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.090
 We're going to talk
about how we write songs,

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:32.710
 How to get the best out of
everyone without dominating,

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.010
 We're going to break down some
of the most classic Metallica

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:36.700
songs,

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.100
 and we're going
to give you a bad---

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.840
performance.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.060
 As far as lyrics
go, I will have

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.270
spaces where I need a
vocal, and then I'll

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.570
search through some
of my one liners,

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.390
and go, "oh,
rhythmically, that fits.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:56.605
So I'll plug that in and
start building around that."

00:00:56.605 --> 00:00:59.240
When you're in a band,
communication is key.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.590
If you have a problem, talk
about it with a level head.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:07.990
 A mantra for us is "whatever's
best for the project."

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:11.290
That helps to quell the ego.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:15.460
In the early days, there
was so much just "pch."

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.770
 There's been times
when it's been fractured

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:22.000
and it looks like we were
on the verge of breaking up.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.240
 30 years ago, we'd come off
stage and we'd go you "f-----

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.650
that up and you f----- that up."

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.690
Nowadays we'd go, "ha, ha, ha.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:29.860
That was really funny.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.510
That was a crazy variation."

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.960
At the end of the day, it's
a f------ rock-and-roll song.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.570
When you're in a band, the
commitment is to music,

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.180
but after a while, you
realize it's a real commitment

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.200
to each other.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.270
That's what being in
a band's all about,

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.432
coming together to
create great things.

00:01:46.432 --> 00:01:47.890
 If there's anything
in what we're

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.560
about to share that in
any way inspires you,

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.720
influences you, makes
you want to jump

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.720
into any creative endeavor,
particularly music,

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.130
then we've done our job.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.150
 I'm James Hetfield.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.590
 I'm Robert Trujillo.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:01.910
 I'm Kirk Hammett.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.080
 I'm Lars Ulrich.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.420
 We are Metallica, and
this is MasterClass.

